+
+
+Kernel package integration
+==========================
+The kernel packages install napctl in /usr/bin and the units in
+/usr/lib/systemd/system. Installation enables nap-state.service and
+nap-state-save.timer. They restore at boot, save at shutdown, and save every
+30 minutes. Units skip kernels without /sys/devices/system/cpu/nap/weights;
+enabling them before the first reboot is expected and does not change the
+governor, its learning settings, sysctl, LRU Marie, zswap, or swapfiles.
+
+State lives in /var/lib/nap (root-only, persistent on SteamOS). Compatible
+records restore per CPU; damaged, incompatible or foreign-machine state is
+ignored and the previous snapshot is tried. Manual commands and timer saves
+share a lock so they cannot race on the kernel's weights_cpu selector. A new
+snapshot is fully written before rotating the previous one. Tool upgrades
+start existing units without restarting and reloading an older snapshot.
+
+Check after booting the new kernel:
+  cat /sys/devices/system/cpu/nap/version
+  systemctl status nap-state.service nap-state-save.timer
+  systemctl list-timers nap-state-save.timer
+  sudo napctl save
+  sudo napctl info /var/lib/nap/state
+
+Open /usr/share/nap/napviz.html in a browser. The production build regenerates
+it from the exact kernel model; it works offline and runs no background server.
+To compare learned models, export to an ordinary file and drop it on the page:
+  sudo napctl export /tmp/nap-state
+  sudo chown "$(id -un):$(id -gn)" /tmp/nap-state
+
+This preserves learning and provides diagnostics; it does not establish an FPS
+gain. No additional training is run outside the existing governor.
+Source commit and original file hashes are recorded in UPSTREAM.json.
